<%# 003 FR-011: every active wish, with enough of each wisher's own locker details
    for a reader to judge whether a swap is worth proposing. The viewer's own wish
    is listed like anyone else's. %>
<%# 017 FR-009/FR-018: the filters drive this frame rather than the whole page, so
    a filter change replaces the list and leaves the declare panel above it — and
    the viewer's place on the page — exactly where they were. data-turbo-action
    "advance" is what still puts the filtered address in the history, so the view
    can be shared and reached with back and forward.

    The id lives on the frame rather than on the card inside it: an id cannot be in
    two places, and accessibility_test.rb and motion_test.rb both match on it. %>
<turbo-frame id="locker-wish-list" data-turbo-action="advance"
             data-controller="frame-history">
  <div class="card stack-tight">
    <h2 class="card-title">Everyone looking for a locker</h2>

    <%# 017 FR-001: the filters sit with the list they narrow, well clear of the
        panel where the viewer declares their own wish, so the two cannot be
        mistaken for one another.

        FR-002: the labels are the list's own column headings below, so the two
        axes are named on screen the way the rows already name them. The parameter
        is called current_floor; the viewer only ever sees "Their floor". %>
    <div id="locker-wish-filters" class="filter-bar">
      <% { looking_for: "Looking for floor", current_floor: "Their floor" }.each do |axis, label| %>
        <%= render "locker_wishes/floor_filter", filters: @floor_filters, axis: axis, label: label %>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <%# 017 FR-019: saving or cancelling a wish is a round trip through #create or
        #destroy, so the filters in force survive it only by travelling with the
        form and coming back on the redirect.
        Those two forms live in the declare panel, which is deliberately outside
        this frame (FR-009) and therefore never re-rendered when a filter changes —
        fields placed inside them would still hold whatever was in force when the
        page first loaded. So the fields live here, where every filter change
        refreshes them, and attach themselves to their forms by id. The HTML `form`
        attribute is what makes that legal, and it costs no JavaScript.
        Sent at the top level, never inside locker_wish: nothing here may reach the
        wish record. %>
    <% @floor_filters.each do |axis, filter| %>
      <% next unless filter.filtering? %>
      <% [ LockerWishesController::DECLARE_FORM_ID, LockerWishesController::CANCEL_FORM_ID ].each do |form_id| %>
        <%= hidden_field_tag axis, filter.selection, id: nil, form: form_id %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <% if @locker_wishes.any? %>
      <%# 012 FR-005: below the breakpoint this stops being a table and becomes one
          stacked card per record — every field labelled from its data-label, the
          row's action right there, nothing behind a sideways swipe. Above it, the
          table it has always been, which is why .table-scroll stays: at that width
          a long row is still allowed its own scroll container, and the page still
          is not.

          The explicit roles are what make the restack safe. Changing an element's
          display drops its implicit table role, so restating the roles keeps the
          accessibility tree correct in both forms (FR-005d). Above the breakpoint
          they duplicate what the element already provides, harmlessly. %>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table" role="table">
          <thead>
            <tr role="row">
              <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Looking for floor</th>
              <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Person</th>
              <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Their floor</th>
              <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Their locker</th>
              <%# 004 FR-001: the swap starts here, on the row of the person being
                  asked. %>
              <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Swap</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>

          <tbody>
            <% @locker_wishes.each do |wish| %>
              <% row_id = "locker-wish-row-#{wish.user_id}" %>
              <tr id="<%= row_id %>" role="row">
                <td id="<%= row_id %>-floor" class="strong" role="cell" data-label="Looking for floor"><%= wish.floor %></td>
                <td id="<%= row_id %>-person" role="cell" data-label="Person"><%= wish.user.email %></td>

                <%# Edge Case: never having saved a floor is distinct from having no
                    locker, and neither is an error — both are plain status text. %>
                <td id="<%= row_id %>-current-floor" role="cell" data-label="Their floor" class="<%= "detail-value-empty" if wish.user.saved_floor.blank? %>">
                  <%= wish.user.saved_floor.presence || "Not set" %>
                </td>

                <td id="<%= row_id %>-current-locker" role="cell" data-label="Their locker" class="<%= "detail-value-empty" if wish.user.saved_locker_number.blank? %>">
                  <%= wish.user.saved_locker_number.presence || "No locker assigned" %>
                </td>

                <%# 004: every reason a swap cannot be proposed is said plainly on
                    the row itself, rather than offering a control that is only
                    going to be refused (FR-002, FR-004, FR-018). %>
                <td id="<%= row_id %>-swap" role="cell" data-label="Swap">
                  <%# 018 FR-006/FR-010: a sibling of whatever else this cell shows,
                      never a replacement — it sits alongside "Proposal pending" or
                      the "Propose swap" button just as easily as it sits alone.
                      Never on the viewer's own row, whatever the floors compare as. %>
                  <% if wish.user_id != current_user.id && wish.reciprocal_match?(@viewer_current_floor, @viewer_wish_floor) %>
                    <span class="badge badge-success">It's a match!</span>
                  <% end %>
                  <% if wish.user_id == current_user.id %>
                    <span class="meta">This is you</span>
                  <% elsif @viewer_in_progress %>
                    <span class="meta">You already have an exchange in progress</span>
                  <% elsif @pending_recipient_ids.include?(wish.user_id) %>
                    <span class="badge badge-info">Proposal pending</span>
                  <% else %>
                    <%# 017 FR-014: the one form inside the frame, and the only one
                        that has to break out of it. Its redirect goes to this very
                        screen, which contains the frame — so without turbo_frame
                        "_top" Turbo would render that redirect into the frame, the
                        "Swap proposal sent." toast would never appear, and the
                        address would lose both filter selections. %>
                    <%= button_to "Propose swap", locker_swap_proposals_path,
                          params: { locker_swap_proposal: { recipient_id: wish.user_id } },
                          class: "btn btn-primary btn-sm",
                          form: { data: { turbo_submits_with: "Proposing…", turbo_frame: "_top" } } %>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    <% elsif @floor_filters.each_value.any?(&:filtering?) %>
      <%# 017 FR-016: an empty result from filtering is an ordinary outcome, and it
          has to say so in its own words. Its own id, not this one with different
          text: a single id would let a regression satisfy the selector while
          saying the wrong thing. Both filters stay above, still showing what is in
          force, so either can be relaxed from here (FR-015). %>
      <p id="locker-wish-list-no-match" class="empty-state">
        No wish matches these filters.
      </p>
    <% else %>
      <%# Acceptance Scenario 2: nobody looking is an ordinary state to report. %>
      <p id="locker-wish-list-empty" class="empty-state">
        Nobody is looking for a locker right now.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</turbo-frame>
